## 1. Exact theorem and dependencies

Use a canonical contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal triangle-free
global minimizer, retaining every source point. Work in the full-core near-disk
branch, for sufficiently large diameter D. Let B be the q actual loaded vertices,
F their convex hull, m_B its number of nonunit sides, Q the bounded contact-face
excess, and k=d_H+s_Z. Make these additional source hypotheses:

1. There is exactly one unloaded positive-contact component I.
2. Every b in B has at least one unit neighbor in I.
3. All points of I belong to one **actual** chart
   Lambda=t+Z u+Z v, with |u|=|v|=1 and u.v=1/2-epsilon,
   where 0<epsilon<=1/50.

No hypothesis identifies the loaded points themselves with this chart.
Contacts between loaded points are allowed, with their original positive
weights. Contact-isolated source points remain in the full configuration.

**Theorem SC.** With u_B=x_B-m_B as in Section 63,

\[
 2k+u_B\le4,\qquad
 q\le2m_B+36D^{2/3}+240D\epsilon+129.                 \tag{SC.1}
\]

In particular, along any such sequence with epsilon -> 0,

\[
 m_B\ge q/2-o(D).                                  \tag{SC.2}
\]

The subcase m_B=0 is impossible already by the finite face ledger.
The subcase m_B<=1 is impossible for all sufficiently large members of a
sequence with epsilon -> 0, because canonical q>=h>=ceil(D).

Canonical inputs, used at their exact scopes, are:

- [C-ZRF](../targets/target-c/57-zr-zf-rigidity-audit.md): mixed rigidity and
  positive-core ledgers; the affine kernel is not extended to isolates.
- [Section 63](../targets/target-c/63-loaded-hull-slack-audit.md):
  e_BB=Q+2k+u_B+c_B and x_B>=m_B.
- [C-COMP-DEATOMIZATION](../targets/target-c/75-all-phase-component-deatomization-audit.md):
  c_I+s_C=m_B+k-2 when c_B=0.
- [C-HARMONIC-CARRIER-QUAD](../targets/target-c/82-harmonic-cell-near-quadrangulation-audit.md),
  Lemma 82.1/Corollary 82.2: convex local faces and strict unit rhombi.
- [C-FULLCORE-LOADED-CHORD](target_c_fullcore_loaded_chord_elimination_2026-09-14.md):
  c_B=0 and e_BB=q-m_B in this branch.
- [Theory 01](../theory/01-local-structure.md): actual diameter endpoint support,
  positive unloaded equilibrium, and unloaded degree at least three.

The current [single-chart endpoint packet](target_c_diameter_ball_hull_lattice_discrepancy_2026-10-02.md)
motivated the attack. None of its Fourier/lattice capacity claims is a premise:
the exact-chart count needed here has the elementary proof in Section 5.
The nested-bundle, threshold, and midpoint reports were inspected but are not
premises. No new literature theorem is imported.

## 2. Endpoint turning and a quantitative four-point lemma

### Lemma SC.2 — every loaded hull vertex pays at least 1/D turning

For an actual diameter endpoint p with partner y put n=(p-y)/D. For every
other source point z,

\[
 n\cdot(z-p)\le-|z-p|^2/(2D).                       \tag{2}
\]

Let t_- and t_+ be incoming and outgoing unit tangents of the loaded hull
at p, with adjacent side lengths a,b>=1 and exterior turn phi. Then

\[
 n\cdot(t_--t_+)\ge(a+b)/(2D)\ge1/D.
\]

Since |t_--t_+|=2 sin(phi/2), phi>=2 asin(1/(2D))>=1/D.
Consequently an oriented angular window of width eta contains at most
1+D eta loaded hull side directions. The same statement holds for a selected
subset of sides: all intervening turns are still counted. Inequality (2)
also gives strict exposure, so collinear loaded hull vertices do not occur.

### Lemma SC.3 — three unit edges with a nearly unit anchor step

Let l,p,q,a be four distinct unit-separated source points with
|l-p|=|p-q|=|q-a|=1 and |a-l|=1+delta, 0<=delta<=1/25. Then

\[
 |(q-p)-(a-l)|\le4\delta.                          \tag{3}
\]

Proof: translate l=0 and write z=(1+delta)^2-1, d=|p-a|.
Here 1<=d<=2. The intersections of the unit circles centered at p,a have
midpoint c=(p+a)/2 and height h=sqrt(1-d^2/4). With the first coordinate
parallel to p-a and the second chosen so c has nonnegative second coordinate,
write c=(A,B). Direct calculation gives

\[
 A=-z/(2d),\qquad B^2=h^2+z/2-A^2.
\]

Since 0<=z<1/10 and d>=1, B>=h. If h<=1/4, both intersections have norm
at most z/2+2h+sqrt(z/2)<1, contradicting separation from l. Thus h>1/4.
Also 0<=B-h=(z/2-A^2)/(B+h)<=z. The low intersection (A,B-h) has norm
at most 3z/2<1 and cannot be q. The high intersection is q; the two
intersections sum to p+a. Hence the error in (3) equals the norm of the low
intersection and is at most 3z/2<4delta. The z=0 case has zero error. QED.

The unit direction of q-p therefore differs in angle from that of a-l by
at most 10delta: their unit-vector distance is at most 5delta, and
2 asin(5delta/2)<=10delta in the stated range.

In the chart of Section 1 the vectors of length at most 1+epsilon are
exactly +/-u, +/-v, +/- (u-v). To check this, the Gram eigenvalue bound
|i u+j v|^2>=(i^2+j^2)/2 reduces this range to i^2+j^2<=2. The vector
u+v has length sqrt(3-2epsilon)>1+epsilon. Also
|u-v|=sqrt(1+2epsilon)<=1+epsilon.

Thus the loaded unit edges whose two chosen chart ports have one of these
six anchor steps lie in six angular windows of width 20epsilon. Lemma SC.2
bounds their number S by

\[
 S\le6+120D\epsilon.                               \tag{4}
\]

This estimate applies also when additional, unselected contacts exist.

## 3. One component makes the face charge injective

Temporarily remove the loaded-loaded edges, leaving G_0 on I union B.
Because I is connected and every loaded vertex has a port into I, deleting
any loaded vertex leaves G_0 connected. Each loaded vertex therefore has
one occurrence on the outer walk, in hull cyclic order. The banks between
successive loaded occurrences contain no other loaded vertex.

Adding the hull sides to G_0 cuts off the corresponding bank pockets.
These pockets have disjoint interiors and each has exactly one hull side.
Here is the separation argument for the other bounded faces. Lemma 82.1
applied with all loaded-loaded edges removed makes every old bounded face
of G_0 a simple convex polygon. If one such polygon touched two or more
loaded hull vertices, each of its cyclic arcs between consecutive loaded
vertices would contain an I vertex, because G_0 has no loaded-loaded edge.
The polygon touches the boundary of F at those loaded vertices. Its
different arcs belong to different complementary caps: a path in F joining
two of those arcs outside the face would have to pass through a touched
hull vertex or enter the face. This is the Jordan separation of a disk by
an interior crosscut, applied successively to the touched hull vertices.
An I path may do neither: it avoids B and the interior of every graph face.
Thus I would be disconnected. Every bounded G_0 face has at most one B
vertex. Adding one hull side cuts off the corresponding bank, whose path
contains only its two terminal B vertices. Adding other hull sides cannot
alter an already bounded pocket. Unloaded articulation occurrences need
not be identified. Lemma 82.1 makes each resulting face cycle simple and
convex.

In particular each actual loaded unit hull side borders its own bounded
contact face, containing precisely its two loaded endpoints. Two different
such sides cannot charge the same face. A quadrilateral of this kind has
two remaining vertices a,b in I, and is a unit rhombus. Its loaded side is
parallel, with the same unit length, to the chart contact ab. For the strict
chart angle in Section 1, its only unit vectors are +/-u and +/-v.
A strictly convex polygon has at most one side in each oriented direction.
Let t be the number of these boundary quadrilaterals. Then t<=4.

Every other loaded unit side charges a different face of degree at least
five. Define the exact nonnegative remainder

\[
 R=\sum_{\text{nonquadrilateral faces not charged to a loaded unit side}}
       (\deg f-4)
   +\sum_{\text{charged nonquadrilateral faces}}(\deg f-5).
\]

Consequently Q=e_BB-t+R. Section 63, with c_B=0, gives

\[
 R=t-2k-u_B,\qquad 2k+u_B\le t\le4.                 \tag{5}
\]

Thus R<=4. At most four charged faces have degree >=6; at most four
nonquadrilateral faces are not charged to loaded unit sides.
If m_B=0, Section 75 gives k=s_C+3>=3, contradicting (5).

## 4. Multiple ports and regular pentagons

Let T count loaded vertices with at least two neighbors in I. The sector
between two consecutive inward ports at such a vertex borders a bounded
face of G_0, so it has no loaded hull side and no other loaded vertex.
If this face is a quadrilateral, its three I vertices are chart points,
and the parallelogram identity puts its loaded vertex in Lambda exactly.
If all its available sectors are nonquadrilateral, assign one such face
to the vertex. This assignment is injective, and (5) allows at most four.
Therefore T is at most the number of loaded chart points plus four.

Discard from the loaded unit edges those incident to one of these T
vertices, those with quadrilateral or degree >=6 charged face, and those
with the short anchor steps in (4). At most

\[
 B_{bad}\le2T+8+S                                  \tag{6}
\]

edges are discarded. Each remaining edge is a regular pentagon side,
with boundary p,q,b,c,a,p, where a,b,c belong to I and a,b are the
unique chart ports of p,q. The two chart edges from a to b through c
have axis directions +/-u or +/-v. Equal-axis steps would make c a
straight unloaded face vertex. Positive equilibrium would then force all
its neighbors onto that line, violating unloaded degree >=3. Backtracking
would repeat a face vertex. The opposite-axis sum u-v is already removed.
Thus, with the boundary orientation retained, every regular edge has

\[
 l(q)-l(p)\in\{d,-d\},\qquad d=u+v,
 \quad |d|=\sqrt{3-2\epsilon}>5/3.                 \tag{7}
\]

### A further push: every consecutive regular pair spends a fixed hull turn

Write p_{i+1}-p_i=t_i, |t_i|=1, and w_i=p_i-l(p_i), |w_i|=1.
If two successive anchor steps are both d (or both -d), then
w_{i+1}-w_i=t_i-d and w_{i+2}-w_{i+1}=t_{i+1}-d. The unit-norm
identities imply

\[
 w_{i+1}\cdot(t_i-t_{i+1})
  =\tfrac12(|t_i-d|^2+|t_{i+1}-d|^2)
  \ge(|d|-1)^2>1/2.                                \tag{8}
\]

Hence the hull turn there is greater than 1/2 radian. If the anchor
steps have opposite signs, l(p_i)=l(p_{i+2}). The four contacts form
a strict unit rhombus on p_i,p_{i+1},p_{i+2},l(p_i), so the exterior
hull turn at p_{i+1} is greater than pi/3, again greater than 1/2.
There are at most twelve consecutive regular pairs on the whole hull.

Removing m_B nonunit sides and B_bad discarded unit sides leaves at
most m_B+B_bad+1 regular runs (the extra one covers a cyclic run).
The number of regular edges is their number of runs plus their number
of consecutive pairs. Thus

\[
 e_{BB}\le m_B+2B_{bad}+13
          \le m_B+4T+2S+29.                         \tag{9}
\]

The earlier, weaker attempt used only the impossibility of three
constant-sign anchor steps, and gave m_B>=q/3-o(D). Identity (8)
repairs that loss: even a pair of such steps consumes a fixed turn.

## 5. Exact-chart capacity without a Fourier premise

Let N loaded vertices belong exactly to Lambda. Their convex hull has
perimeter <=pi D and distinct oriented lattice side vectors. The lattice
has minimum spacing one. Disjoint radius-1/2 disks show that the number
of lattice vectors of norm <=r is at most 4(r+1/2)^2<=9r^2 for r>=1.
For N>=18 set r=sqrt(N/18). At most N/2 of the distinct side vectors
have norm <=r. Therefore

\[
 \pi D\ge N^{3/2}/(2\sqrt{18}).
\]

Using 2 sqrt(18) pi<27 gives N<=9D^(2/3). The smaller values of N,
including a segment or singleton hull, are covered by
N<=18+9D^(2/3). Hence

\[
 T\le22+9D^{2/3}.                                  \tag{10}
\]

Combine e_BB=q-m_B, (4), (9), and (10) to get (SC.1).
This count is about actual endpoints in an actual chart; it does not
round off-chart ports, assume a uniform multiplier, or require global
optimality of a numerical example.

## 6. Push to the remaining source branch, and an exact regression

The resulting collar is asymptotically separated into isolated loaded unit
edges and nonunit hull sides, with k<=2. The canonical component identity
gives s_C=m_B+k-3. Therefore the remaining escape is a **linear nonunit-side
and pinned-surplus collar**, not a private diameter-tree leaf.
For comparison with the exact canonical quantity, the loaded-chord ledger
gives mu_D D=q+m_B-k-6 here. Section 75 gives liminf q/D>=2rho,
rho=cos(rho), so this conditional branch has liminf mu_D>=3rho.
There is no conflicting upper bound; the substantive result is the forced
nonunit-side population and the exclusion of the small-gap collars.

The obvious stronger assertion that a long-anchor unit edge is impossible
even with actual diameter endpoints is false. Put

\[
 h=\sqrt{\sqrt3/2},\quad
 l=(-\sqrt3/2,0),\ a=(\sqrt3/2,0),\quad
 p=(-1/2,h),\ q=(1/2,h),
\]

and include p'=(-1/2,h-10), q'=(1/2,h-10).
The diameter is sqrt(101), attained exactly by p q' and q p'.
The unit contacts are l p, p q, q a, and p' q'; all other distances
are strictly larger than one. The source is triangle-free. Both p,q
are actual diameter endpoints and their anchors are in the exact
triangular chart with u=(sqrt3/2,1/2), v=(sqrt3/2,-1/2): a-l=u+v.
Thus a long-anchor loaded dimer is genuine finite source geometry.

This regression omits positive unloaded equilibrium (l,a are leaves),
one connected unloaded component, near-optimal quadratic population,
the strict chart angle, and global optimality. It refutes only the
geometry-only deletion of long-anchor dimers, not Theorem SC.

The integer ledgers also admit arbitrarily large formal high-surplus
states after (SC.2): for integer j>=10 take D=j, q=3j, m_B=2j,
k=2, d_H=1, s_Z=1, c_I=1, s_C=2j-1, u_B=0, c_B=0,
x_B=2j, Q=j-4, e_BB=j, h=3j-1. All the exact ledgers above hold,
and mu_D=5-8/j. These are **formal ledger regressions**, not embedded
sources or global minimizers. They demonstrate why the new charge alone
does not contradict the high-surplus branch.

